    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d989">

                <pb facs="1029" n="909" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg909"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">823.00 Revolutions/119: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Peru</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dearing</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-03-01T08:00:00-05:00">March 1, 1931—8
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 2:15 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>72. Juan O’Connor, chief of Flying Service, has seized two Johnson-Shippee planes
                    and ordered pilots Hay, Shippee and Johnson, over their protest, to make a
                    flight with military officers to Pisco. I am protesting at once to Foreign
                    Minister and asking release of planes and pilots. Major Allen<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d989-f1"/> is doing the same to Juan.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dearing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d989-f1" n="5" xml:id="d989-f1-content">
                <p>Major C. J. Allen, Military Attaché.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
